node_modules/
.env

# public/ is the staged asset tree. Every byte of it is copied out of
# ../archcanvas-promo-video by scripts/sync-assets.mjs and is regenerable, so it
# is not tracked. src/data/assets.json IS tracked -- it is the ledger that says
# which bytes the film was cut from, and it can be checked in a diff.
# public/ is the staged asset tree. Most of it is copied out of
# ../archcanvas-promo-video by scripts/sync-assets.mjs, or cut from the raw OBS
# takes in D:ideos, and all of that is regenerable and uninteresting in a
# diff -- so it is not tracked. src/data/assets.json IS tracked; it is the
# ledger that says which bytes the film was cut from.
#
# The generated audio is the exception, and the line is this: material that was
# COPIED from somewhere else or can be RE-RENDERED stays out; material that was
# GENERATED HERE and cannot be reproduced comes in.
#
# The twelve files below cost API credits and are not deterministic -- asking
# ElevenLabs for the same sound effect twice does not return the same bytes.
# They are 1.04 MB in total. Leaving them untracked meant a fresh clone could
# not render the film at all, and the loss would have been silent: a wipe of
# public/audio followed by a sync reported success with the cues gone from disk
# AND from the ledger. sync-assets.mjs now refuses that outright, but the real
# fix is that these bytes live somewhere durable.
public/*
!public/.gitkeep
!public/audio/
public/audio/*
!public/audio/vo-*.mp3
!public/audio/sfx-detent.mp3
!public/audio/sfx-final-settle.mp3
!public/audio/sfx-paper-rush.mp3
!public/audio/sfx-room-air.mp3
!public/audio/sfx-manifest.json

# Raw audio sources (a ~200s SUNO take is ~5MB and is regenerable input).
audio/source/*
!audio/source/.gitkeep

# Renders.
#
# An 85s 1080p master is ~90MB per aspect ratio and git keeps every version
# forever. Four formats times a few review renders is a permanent gigabyte in a
# repo people clone to read a few thousand lines of TypeScript. Masters ship as
# GitHub Release assets.
#
# The sidecars are text and they are the reviewable record of what the film
# says, so they stay.
out/*
!out/*.srt
!out/*.vtt
